Overlay patch 0055: the unguarded Metal framebuffer allocations (VR R9 item
1b). The other half of the rainbow work — the eye-slot handoff — lives in
overlay 0044 rev4, because it edits code 0044 itself adds and a patch that
lands inside another patch's hunk context breaks apply-overlay.sh's
reverse-probe idempotence check. Read 0044 rev4's header first; this one is
the memory-pressure half of the same story.

`UpdateFramebufferParameters` calls `mDevice->newTexture()` five times and
checks NONE of them, while `tex.width/height` are stamped with the requested
size regardless. Under memory pressure `newTexture` returns nil — this repo
already knows that and already guards the same call in the TEXTURE UPLOAD path
(overlay 0021 rev2, "UploadTexture: newTexture failed"). The FRAMEBUFFER path
is where the 83 MB objects live and it had no guard at all.

The failure mode is worse than a dropped frame: the incumbent texture has
ALREADY been released by then, the new pointer is null, and the size fields say
the resize succeeded — so `diff` reads false from then on and IT NEVER RETRIES.
One failed allocation and that framebuffer is dead for the rest of the session,
with the compositor left holding whatever it last saw.

At 100% render quality on device the VR attachments alone are ~830 MB: four eye
slots at 5087x4081, colour AND Depth32Float, plus a HUD framebuffer at the same
extent. That is exactly the size of thing a Siri screenshot's transient spike
can push over — which is the user's new evidence, and it is why the artefacts get
likelier at the screenshot moment even at LOW quality.

THE FIX, and the ordering is the whole of it: allocate the replacement FIRST,
and release the incumbent only once every allocation for that framebuffer has
succeeded. On failure — log loudly (this project does not do silent skips),
bump `gSohIosGfxAllocFails`, and leave the old texture AND the old size fields
alone, so the framebuffer stays VALID (stale size, real contents) instead of
becoming garbage, and `diff` stays true so the next frame tries again.

`gSohIosGfxFbBytes` totals the live framebuffer attachment bytes, recomputed
whenever one changes size, so `vr contract` can report what is actually
resident at the moment the device misbehaves.

REV2 (review finding, 2026-08-22). rev1 armed the retry for the COLOUR
attachment only. The colour block stamps `tex.width/height` the moment its own
allocation succeeds — before the depth block runs — so a frame where colour
succeeded and depth then failed left the function in exactly the dead state the
patch was written to prevent: next call `diff` is false (the size fields match
the request), `fb.mHasDepthBuffer` is already true, the depth guard never fires
again, and the framebuffer keeps a new-size colour attachment bound against an
old-size depth attachment for the rest of the session. Fix: the depth guard now
also compares the incumbent depth texture's OWN width/height against the
request. `MTL::Texture` carries its extent and this file already reads it that
way in the floating-frame path, so no new struct state is needed and the retry
stays armed through any partial failure, in either order. Logging and the
`gSohIosGfxAllocFails` telemetry are unchanged.

REV3 (VR R13 item 1a, from docs/vr-r13-maps/allocfail-quiesce-map.md). rev2 kept
the retry armed but never reasoned about the FRAMES IN BETWEEN. The depth guard's
bare `return` jumps over the depth-ATTACHMENT fixup at the end of the function —
and by then the COLOUR attachment has already been swapped into
`fb.mRenderPassDescriptor` at the new size and `tex.width/height` stamped. What
is left behind is a descriptor holding a new-size colour attachment against an
old-size depth attachment: Metal rejects it, and `StartDrawToFramebuffer` has no
nil-encoder guard, so it is an NSInternalInconsistencyException (SIGABRT, or
SIGTRAP through the Metal validation layer) or a nil-encoder dereference
(SIGSEGV). That is the user's third-map-load crash and, with the mirror in the
live set, very likely the Siri-screenshot one too.

rev3 does three things:
  1. On depth failure the descriptor is made STRUCTURALLY CONSISTENT — the depth
     attachment is dropped outright — so nothing invalid can reach Metal.
  2. That framebuffer is marked SKIPPED, and `StartDrawToFramebuffer` refuses to
     open a pass on it (loudly, bounded) until an `UpdateFramebufferParameters`
     call completes for it. A frame of missing content beats a crash, and the
     retry is still armed by the rev2 depth-size clause.
  3. `gSohIosGfxFbFailIn` — THE MISSING RED CONTROL. Until now nothing in the
     tree could drive `gSohIosGfxAllocFails` above zero, so every green run
     reporting `alloc_fails=0` was a tautology rather than evidence (trap D11).
     The knob is `vr set fbfail <n>`: fail the Nth next attachment allocation.
     It lives in `SohIosShell.m` (defined on every target — the Fast3D overlay's
     externs are strong and `__IOS__` is defined on iPhone too) and is consumed
     here.
`gSohIosGfxFbSkips` counts the refusals; `vr contract` reports it.


